AB  - This paper introduces a physics-informed model-based offline meta-reinforcement learning framework for autonomous underwater vehicle (AUV) trajectory tracking control under ocean current disturbances. The proposed approach employs a physics-informed neural network to develop a long-term predictive model of AUV motion in the horizontal plane, which acts as an interactive environment for training the controller. By integrating a probabilistic context inference mechanism into offline conservative policy optimization, the controller gains the ability to adapt rapidly to disturbances caused by ocean currents. The two-stage control architecture and well-designed Markov decision processes are adopted to streamline controller training. Simulation results demonstrate that with just 2000 motion data samples, the system achieves effective trajectory tracking even under unknown ocean currents. © 2025 IEEE.
