AB  - Recent world foundation models can synthesize plausible future observations from text, image, video, and action-conditioned inputs. However, generating a future video is not equivalent to providing an interactive environment in which users or agents can act, return, inspect, revise, and evaluate state changes over long horizons. This paper proposes a state-managed framework for transforming Cosmos-like world prediction models into task-oriented interactive simulation systems. The proposed framework focuses on the visual world-prediction component of Cosmos-like systems and separates model-level generation from runtime state management: prediction modules generate future observations, while explicit state managers, action interfaces, visual-language parsers, consistency controllers, and feedback loops maintain object persistence, spatial coherence, and action-state validity. By positioning Genie-like generative interactive environments and Cosmos-like physical-AI world predictors as complementary but distinct paradigms, the paper clarifies the missing middle layer required to convert world prediction into controllable simulation. A compact research prototype and evaluation plan are presented for validating action success, state-observation consistency, object persistence, spatial coherence, and long-horizon stability. © 2026 IEEE.
