AB  - The creation of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) needs more than task-specific intelligent agents because it requires agents with world perception and abstract system understanding and continuous learning capabilities and experience recall and logical reasoning together with behavioral adaptation across different situations. The proposed framework creates a single structure for Embodied AGI through the combination of World Models with Differentiable Memory together with Neurosymbolic Reasoning methods. These three elements establish ground for an intelligent agent which exhibits cognitive and reflective response capabilities. The system foundation contains a World Model based on DreamerV3 that develops reduced environment representations for latent spaces. Through internal simulation capabilities and making actions before execution the agent maintains flexibility beyond pure direct encounters with the environment. Through mental simulations the system performs planning and enables exploration and generalization with superior speed using less actual world observations. The Differentiable Neural Computer (DNC) serves as an external memory system when used alongside this architecture. Neural network algorithms face limitations because they lose essential details obtained in prior encounters. The DNC solves this issue through an agentcontrolled memory matrix that allows explicit and structured reads and writes that support differentiable operations. Through this mechanism agents develop the ability to access particular previous episodes alongside specific procedures along with goals and strategies for deploying known information in related new scenarios which demonstrates true intelligent capability. Tracking predictions together with memory systems do not equate to genuine general intelligence. High-level reasoning requires the integration of Neurosymbolic Reasoning Module which connects subsymbolic latent spaces with symbolic logical inference. The module uses extracted abstractions (including objects and relations together with rules) from latent embeddings and environment history. The module applies learned logic functions with learning mechanisms to execute causal reasoning operations alongside hierarchical planning functions and task decomposition procedures. The agent uses this capability to understand its decision-making and maintain logical belief consistency as well as fix knowledge inaccuracies during contradiction detection which solves the "black-box"problem with deep learning. The integrated perception-simulation-memory-reasoning-action loop contains these modules which lets the agent perform meaningfully in complex dynamically changing partially observable environments and reinforcement learning for dynamic resource allocation. The system undergoes evaluations in virtual environments where agents execute tasks that need spatial thinking along with memory-based navigation and language-based goal interpretation together with the ability to adapt to unfamiliar conditions.The hybrid architectural design delivers consistently better performance to traditional models regarding both sample efficiency and task adaptability and interpretability and reasoning consistency. Through its functioning ability the system exhibits intellectual behaviors that include future projection abilities and diagrammatic symbol processing together with stored memory retrieval and fast situational adaptation.The described framework advances AGI development through a demonstration that shows how integrated imagination with memory systems and reasoning mechanisms inside embodied grounded agents leads to effective generalization and traceable performance. The proposed research framework establishes fundamental principles for future studies about curriculum learning and extended memory storage procedures and physical robots within this system. Artificial agents develop progress in their ability to both learn from and understand the world. © 2026 IEEE.
